README Doc

This document provides the instructions for setting up and running the required
Rust programs: helloworld.rs, lp1.rs, and lp2.rs. It includes necessary steps and
checks for required software installation and compilation instructions.

Required Software:
To compile and execute Rust programs, you will need to have Rust installed on your
system. Rust includes both the compiler (rustc) and Cargo, which is Rust's package
manager and build system. Below are the steps to install Rust based on your operating
system.

Installing Rust
The recommended way to install Rust is through (rustup), which is Rust's offical toolchain
installer. This method ensures that you always have the latest stable version and allows for
easy updates.

To install Rust using rustup, open a terminal and run the following command:

curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh

This command is provided by the official Rust website and downloads the Rust toolchain
installer. If you would like to review the official installation documentation for yourself
or just learn more about (rustup), then visit:

https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install

Once the installation is complete, restart your terminal and verify that Rust has been
installed correctly by running:

rustc --version
cargo --version

This should display the installed version of Rust and Cargo. If you see an error like "command
not found", you may need to restart your terminal or manually set your environment variables by running:

source $HOME/.cargo/env


Alternative Installation for macOS users (Homebrew)
Mac users who prefer homebrew can install Rust by running:

brew install rust

However, using (rustup) is still the recommended option because it will allow you to have better version
control and updates.


Text Editor or IDE
While you can write Rust programs in any text editor, using a code editor makes it easier to write, debug,
and format code properly. This is especially useful for new programmers or anyone that's new to Rust! Some
recommended editors include:

VS Code (Recommended & what I use!) - You can install the Rust Analyzer extension for syntax highlighting
and error checking.

JetBrains CLion - Rust support via plugin and popular IDE.

Many other text editors and IDEs can be used with Rust, you might just have to install extensions or manual
setups within them.


Command Line Interface or CLI
Rust programs are compiled and run through a terminal or command prompt. Use the appropriate CLI for your system:

macOS - Use the Terminal application

Windows - Use Command Prompt or you can use Powershell.

Linux - Any terminal shell should work, such as Bash or Zsh.


Compilation Instructions
Rust source code files (.rs files) need to be compiled into an executable before they can be run. 
Compilation is done using the Rust compiler (rustc). Follow these steps for each program:

Compiling helloworld.rs:

rustc helloworld.rs

By default, this creates an executable file named helloworld in the same directory.
To run the executable, use: ./helloworld

Compiling lp1.rs:

rustc lp1.rs

This produces an executable file named lp1.
To run the executable, use: ./lp1

Compiling lp2.rs:

rustc lp2.rs

This produces an executable file named lp2.
To run the executable, use: ./lp2


If you encounter a "command not found" error when running rustc, 
double-check that Rust is installed by running:

rustc --version

If Rust is installed but still not recognized, try running:

source $HOME/.cargo/env

This refreshes the environment variables so that your system can recognize Rust commands.